nodecode-crashtest is a rewrite of salch-cred's crash-test:

  https://github.com/salch-cred/crash-test
  commit 00f30620de92a470721b19a158fe26ceee8b0025 (2026-11-08)
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 visual-gate contributors (LICENSE)

The plugin was 900 lines of Python registering five Hermes hooks and one slash
command. This rewrite keeps what a user gets from it -- the four fault
profiles, the loud/silent safety line, the seed that makes runs comparable,
the scoring table and the one-star cap, the scorecard under the reply, the
saved run and the model comparison, and the practice drill with its known
answer -- in one Common Lisp file against the cell contract.

The five Hermes hooks became the organism's own seams: `pre_llm_call` is the
`:ROUND` point at round one, `pre_tool_call` and `transform_tool_result` are
the two halves of one `:TOOL` hook, and `transform_llm_output` is advice on
the assistant-message record, where the turn's final text is durable and the
scorecard can ride it. `on_session_end` became the round-one handover that
closes a run the turn never replied under, so a run still cannot leak past
its turn.

Left out, and why:

- The HTML report. The rewrite saves each run as one readable Lisp form under
the home's `crash-test/reports/` folder, which is what `/crashtest report`
and `/crashtest compare` read; a browser page of it added a stylesheet, not a
rating.
- The `CRASH_TEST` environment variable. It existed to arm a run from a
scripted Hermes process; `/crashtest start profile seed` is the way in here.
- Hermes' tool names. The read-only list and the read-only `eval` forms are
Nodecode's (`look`, `read`, `websearch`, `(sh "ls -la")`), and the eager
tool set the original exempted (`clarify`, `memory`, `todo`) is replaced by
the session verbs this organism has.

The fault wording, the profiles, the point table and the star thresholds are
the original's, unchanged.
